# Binary assets must never have end-of-line conversion applied.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true in this repo, so without these rules Git falls back to
# content sniffing to decide what is binary. That heuristic is right almost
# always, but "almost" is not a good bet for a font: a single LF rewritten as
# CRLF inside a glyph table corrupts the file, and the damage shows up as a
# failed load on someone else's checkout rather than here.

*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary

*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary

# Files that end up on a Linux host must keep LF endings regardless of
# `core.autocrlf`. A shell script checked out with CRLF fails on the server with
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, and the deploy workflow rsyncs these
# straight from a checkout — so the corruption would only surface on the box it
# breaks. Same reasoning for the nginx template and the env template the deploy
# scripts source with `.`.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.template      text eol=lf
*.inc           text eol=lf
.env.deploy.example text eol=lf
